1
00:00:00,331 --> 00:00:03,212
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what do you mean when you talk about psychologization?

2
00:00:04,033 --> 00:00:07,194
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you recognize yourself doing it, and why is it a problem?

3
00:00:08,815 --> 00:00:28,003
[SPEAKER_02]: So, psychologization, the term I came up with, is basically, it means like, when you are doing, like, self-explaining to yourself, or explaining as to why and how things happen to yourself, to help you cope.

4
00:00:30,642 --> 00:00:33,124
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't help doing it in the beginning.

5
00:00:33,144 --> 00:00:41,009
[SPEAKER_02]: Obviously, the more successful your practice is, the less and less you'll be doing it, and eventually you wouldn't be doing any psychologization, because you're free from suffering.

6
00:00:41,470 --> 00:00:45,552
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's like the the internal mental response to being affected by dukkha.

7
00:00:46,493 --> 00:00:54,038
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, like a really unrestrained person will just act with the body and punch or steal, do stuff to get what they want, so that they don't suffer.

8
00:00:54,738 --> 00:00:57,901
[SPEAKER_02]: Somebody who doesn't do that, but then still affected by

9
00:00:58,810 --> 00:01:01,892
[SPEAKER_02]: The world, internally, they will have to find ways of coping.

10
00:01:02,173 --> 00:01:06,796
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, it's like rationalizations, justifications, explanations.

11
00:01:07,177 --> 00:01:11,500
[SPEAKER_02]: Sometimes, it's not even, like, necessarily that kind of coarse form of avoiding responsibility mentally.

12
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:12,561
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I did it because of this.

13
00:01:12,881 --> 00:01:18,386
[SPEAKER_02]: I know I couldn't, but I did it because this is what happened, and they said this, and this, and therefore, it was deserved.

14
00:01:19,006 --> 00:01:19,887
[SPEAKER_02]: That's already psychological.

15
00:01:19,907 --> 00:01:20,948
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's just like, it's a cover-up.

16
00:01:21,989 --> 00:01:28,294
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if it was the most deserved action that you did, if you're having to explain it to yourself, it's a cover-up.

17
00:01:29,016 --> 00:01:32,717
[SPEAKER_02]: means you're covering up the discomfort of it, the discomfort of responsibility of it.

18
00:01:32,977 --> 00:01:39,359
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's why it's a problem, that's why it's unwholesome, because it always partakes in some degree of ignorance, of avijjā, of a cover-up.

19
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:49,422
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it could even be a subtler form as well, which would be like, you know, like, the whole practice is about understanding,

20
00:01:50,481 --> 00:01:54,765
[SPEAKER_02]: But then, obviously, in order to understand the mind, you need to start to see the mind.

21
00:01:54,825 --> 00:02:02,411
[SPEAKER_02]: In order to see the mind, well, you need to have been practicing the restraint and withdrawal from all the things and actions that you make that obscure the mind.

22
00:02:03,892 --> 00:02:05,053
[SPEAKER_02]: And that takes time to develop.

23
00:02:06,134 --> 00:02:08,936
[SPEAKER_02]: It takes faith to stick to as well, to keep doing it.

24
00:02:09,677 --> 00:02:15,982
[SPEAKER_02]: So then, what people would often kind of become trapped by is that

25
00:02:17,909 --> 00:02:22,492
[SPEAKER_02]: The need to understand things gets conflated with figuring things out.

26
00:02:23,953 --> 00:02:30,458
[SPEAKER_02]: So, collecting the bits of information, making sense out of it in my mind, equals understanding.

27
00:02:31,419 --> 00:02:32,660
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's another psychologicalization.

28
00:02:34,361 --> 00:02:36,863
[SPEAKER_02]: That's another cover-up of sorts.

29
00:02:37,818 --> 00:02:44,322
[SPEAKER_02]: So now, when dukkha happens, well, that's just the phassa that's affected by the, you know, sense objects.

30
00:02:44,602 --> 00:02:49,365
[SPEAKER_02]: This comes together, then this happens, then this is the response, then that's the response.

31
00:02:49,765 --> 00:02:50,025
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

32
00:02:50,985 --> 00:02:51,826
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm cleared.

33
00:02:53,141 --> 00:02:57,543
[SPEAKER_02]: But, again, you can't, as I said, you can't just magically stop doing that.

34
00:02:57,563 --> 00:02:59,463
[SPEAKER_02]: There's going to be some tendency to explain.

35
00:02:59,523 --> 00:03:12,748
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not going to be perfect, but if you keep looking beyond it, as in like, okay, I am still putting pieces together and trying this and trying that, but overall, when I'm touched by suffering, do I need to do anything?

36
00:03:14,549 --> 00:03:20,211
[SPEAKER_02]: Is there any impulse, a compulsion, to explain it to myself?

37
00:03:21,744 --> 00:03:22,404
[SPEAKER_02]: What happened here?

38
00:03:23,105 --> 00:03:23,325
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

39
00:03:24,986 --> 00:03:25,846
[SPEAKER_02]: Why do I have to do that?

40
00:03:27,487 --> 00:03:36,871
[SPEAKER_02]: Why can't I just understand things as they are, without needing to go through the self-explanatory loops?

41
00:03:38,032 --> 00:03:40,033
[SPEAKER_02]: Why can't in the scene just be the scene?

42
00:03:40,393 --> 00:03:42,414
[SPEAKER_02]: What I've seen is just to the extent that it's seen.

43
00:03:43,054 --> 00:03:48,737
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't need to be saying what happened behind the scenes to feel comfortable with this.

44
00:03:50,826 --> 00:03:52,447
[SPEAKER_02]: In perceived, there's just perceived.

45
00:03:52,527 --> 00:03:53,967
[SPEAKER_02]: In the felt, there will be just the felt.

46
00:03:54,968 --> 00:04:03,451
[SPEAKER_02]: Why can't I stay with that the most, the broadest, often most ambiguous, yet, as I said, real as such level?

47
00:04:04,951 --> 00:04:09,113
[SPEAKER_02]: Why do I need... Why do I have to explain my feelings to myself?

48
00:04:10,313 --> 00:04:12,034
[SPEAKER_02]: Why can't I just stop short?

49
00:04:13,774 --> 00:04:15,855
[SPEAKER_02]: Can I recognize stopping short real as such?

50
00:04:17,100 --> 00:04:24,824
[SPEAKER_02]: Or is the impulse to complete the sentence internally, of what has just occurred, taken for granted, and I always just act out of it.

51
00:04:26,524 --> 00:04:32,647
[SPEAKER_02]: So it's like a deeply rooted psychological mental habit, need, that comes from that need to just deal with suffering.

52
00:04:33,808 --> 00:04:36,569
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why it's fundamentally just inseparable from avijjā.

53
00:04:40,290 --> 00:04:45,475
[SPEAKER_00]: So, where would you then start to try to identify?

54
00:04:46,356 --> 00:05:03,192
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I think for anybody who's taken on the precepts, trying to develop restraint and sīla to a certain extent, there is the very easy tendency of whenever you start to suffer, to say, oh, well, it must be because of failure in morality and this or that.

55
00:05:04,853 --> 00:05:08,234
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't seem to me like that's always 100% wrong.

56
00:05:08,274 --> 00:05:09,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it can be accurate.

57
00:05:09,915 --> 00:05:13,836
[SPEAKER_00]: But when does that... Basically, how do you make sure that you're doing that correctly?

58
00:05:13,856 --> 00:05:15,917
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I think that's what I mean, like, stopping the mid-sentence.

59
00:05:16,597 --> 00:05:22,859
[SPEAKER_02]: The first half of the sentence would be just the thoughts that the mind came up with, which often might be even factual.

60
00:05:22,979 --> 00:05:31,542
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what I said, like, in the previous conversations, like, oh, I did that because he said this, and he was deserving it, and he was really unpleasant, and I needed to put him in his place.

61
00:05:31,902 --> 00:05:32,683
[SPEAKER_02]: And that might be 100% true.

62
00:05:35,060 --> 00:05:35,820
[SPEAKER_02]: Those facts.

63
00:05:36,321 --> 00:05:40,403
[SPEAKER_02]: But the fact is, if you acted out of ill will, that's always on you, that part.

64
00:05:41,083 --> 00:05:52,109
[SPEAKER_02]: So, this is the same, like, you start keeping the precepts, start trying to figure things out, but as things arise, as challenges present themselves,

65
00:05:54,533 --> 00:06:06,895
[SPEAKER_02]: You kind of, say, recognize to some extent, like, okay, well, the problem here is maybe, you know, I slipped in the precepts, I acted out, so that's why there's... and it's like, you will, you will feel the line, so to speak.

66
00:06:07,135 --> 00:06:18,518
[SPEAKER_02]: You will see where, where that recognition and that, say, like, thought process comes up to a certain extent, and when it becomes emotionally charged, that's the difference here.

67
00:06:19,618 --> 00:06:20,478
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's like, oh, it's because...

68
00:06:21,595 --> 00:06:25,799
[SPEAKER_02]: But now there is this kind of vacuum left, of unfulfilled pressure.

69
00:06:26,520 --> 00:06:34,808
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, let me repeat this again, the same information, to feed this, pretty much, this pressure that is there.

70
00:06:35,995 --> 00:06:38,716
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's now passing the mid-sentence.

71
00:06:39,176 --> 00:06:39,936
[SPEAKER_02]: Now you're adding out.

72
00:06:39,956 --> 00:06:48,279
[SPEAKER_02]: So it feels... Again, I say mid-sentence because you start from the position of overdoing, making those mistakes, doing psychologizations.

73
00:06:48,319 --> 00:06:52,460
[SPEAKER_02]: But in reality, the extent that it has arisen, it's sufficient enough.

74
00:06:52,520 --> 00:06:53,740
[SPEAKER_02]: It's already a finished sentence.

75
00:06:54,500 --> 00:06:59,762
[SPEAKER_02]: The fact that you feel ambiguous about it, does not make the mental sentence unfinished.

76
00:07:00,630 --> 00:07:04,751
[SPEAKER_02]: That's your own view that paints it that way, and then you keep adding more.

77
00:07:05,311 --> 00:07:07,772
[SPEAKER_02]: But again, the framework will never be satisfied.

78
00:07:09,052 --> 00:07:14,274
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there's never gonna end the proliferation of adding more and needing more information.

79
00:07:14,314 --> 00:07:21,516
[SPEAKER_02]: See, if you never see the problem of that mental habit of psychologizing, it's infinitely hungry.

80
00:07:23,428 --> 00:07:35,458
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, there is no end to then you needing new things, new angles, new interpretations, new explanations, new techniques, new practices to keep feeding that furnace, basically.

81
00:07:36,498 --> 00:07:39,881
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the things become stale if you just repeat the same psychological pattern.

82
00:07:39,921 --> 00:07:41,602
[SPEAKER_02]: They don't offer the relief anymore.

83
00:07:42,543 --> 00:07:45,846
[SPEAKER_02]: So you need to embellish, you need to change, you need to find a different...

84
00:07:46,993 --> 00:07:49,273
[SPEAKER_02]: A different take on those things.

85
00:07:50,054 --> 00:07:55,174
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's never-ending, because again, that's not where the root of the problem was.

86
00:07:55,314 --> 00:08:02,956
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, in terms of... there's one sutta that says, one cannot come to the end of dukkha without understanding the Four Noble Truths.

87
00:08:03,276 --> 00:08:04,536
[SPEAKER_01]: Such a thing is not possible.

88
00:08:05,116 --> 00:08:09,437
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to understand the Four Noble Truths to bring an end to suffering.

89
00:08:10,657 --> 00:08:10,897
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

90
00:08:12,757 --> 00:08:15,818
[SPEAKER_01]: So, one can read there and say, well, I have to now understand

91
00:08:16,739 --> 00:08:16,919
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

92
00:08:17,319 --> 00:08:18,100
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is suffering?

93
00:08:18,120 --> 00:08:19,621
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the cause of suffering?

94
00:08:19,641 --> 00:08:26,846
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, well, the Buddha says this in the Sutta and... Yeah, that's why, again, learning is indispensable.

95
00:08:27,846 --> 00:08:30,068
[SPEAKER_02]: You need to have the right information, the right instruction.

96
00:08:30,088 --> 00:08:31,409
[SPEAKER_02]: We spoke about it so many times.

97
00:08:32,129 --> 00:08:37,453
[SPEAKER_02]: At the same time, like, if you are not transparent enough, as I said, you will

98
00:08:39,155 --> 00:08:44,058
[SPEAKER_02]: Often, confuse the learning for making connections between mental information.

99
00:08:44,578 --> 00:08:48,540
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, sorry, you will confuse understanding for mere learning.

100
00:08:48,580 --> 00:08:53,363
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I know the suttas, I know the Pali, I know all the terms, how they connect, I can explain them.

101
00:08:53,883 --> 00:08:58,085
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, yeah, that's the informational level that is necessary.

102
00:08:58,725 --> 00:09:07,290
[SPEAKER_02]: But does that informational level, or have you also learned about, through information, the necessity of self-transparency and precepts?

103
00:09:08,843 --> 00:09:12,707
[SPEAKER_02]: That can then elevate your merely informational level to the actual practice.

104
00:09:12,928 --> 00:09:13,168
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

105
00:09:13,548 --> 00:09:19,795
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, because if you're truly understanding those terms, you would not be affected by dukkha emotionally.

106
00:09:21,918 --> 00:09:27,624
[SPEAKER_02]: You wouldn't need to do anything on account of the arisen dukkha, because you are not affected by it to begin with.

107
00:09:28,559 --> 00:09:38,667
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if that criteria, if that reference point, that understanding leads to freedom from suffering here and now, at the most deepest existential level, you are free.

108
00:09:40,128 --> 00:09:46,073
[SPEAKER_02]: If that criteria is not known, learned, maintained, discerned, you're not understanding anything.

109
00:09:47,014 --> 00:09:50,877
[SPEAKER_02]: Your understanding is just understanding relative to information that you have.

110
00:09:52,201 --> 00:10:07,950
[SPEAKER_02]: And often, again, as I said, even in my own practice, in my process of, like, learning and collecting, I remember, like, you know, reading this book or that book and the sutra translation and stuff, and often it was just like a, like, almost like a comparative study.

111
00:10:07,970 --> 00:10:13,514
[SPEAKER_02]: And I didn't understand why it was wrong back then, to what extent it was wrong, but I remember I couldn't quite

112
00:10:14,507 --> 00:10:14,927
[SPEAKER_02]: Makes sense.

113
00:10:14,987 --> 00:10:22,433
[SPEAKER_02]: How would this free the liability to suffer here on the, as I said, the most personal level?

114
00:10:23,073 --> 00:10:24,534
[SPEAKER_02]: There was just nothing there.

115
00:10:25,215 --> 00:10:26,395
[SPEAKER_02]: It was just, oh, you just do this.

116
00:10:27,096 --> 00:10:38,224
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it kind of almost became again like another, as I said, mechanical method of just, just, just, just fitting the puzzles of the information, the pieces of connecting, finding angles, writing about it, kind of fleshing it out and so on.

117
00:10:38,564 --> 00:10:39,725
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, but here and now,

118
00:10:40,953 --> 00:10:42,314
[SPEAKER_02]: What is your motivation to do that?

119
00:10:42,574 --> 00:10:47,636
[SPEAKER_02]: Have you included that in your informational Jenga puzzle?

120
00:10:49,457 --> 00:11:02,382
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's like, no, that's like, that's kind of like assumed that those things will just happen by themselves, that, you know, purification of your own relationship with your own mind and your feelings and acting out on the body, speech and mind.

121
00:11:02,422 --> 00:11:06,104
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, oh, so then all the information in the world will not free you from suffering.

122
00:11:07,433 --> 00:11:10,394
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you can read the Pali study suttas all you want.

123
00:11:11,354 --> 00:11:21,717
[SPEAKER_02]: It will take you in the direction of, as I said, informational knowledge that's now filled the place for understanding for you.

124
00:11:22,438 --> 00:11:25,158
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, somebody's gonna think, ah, so I must stop reading.

125
00:11:25,378 --> 00:11:25,659
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

126
00:11:26,419 --> 00:11:36,782
[SPEAKER_02]: You should, by all means, keep reading study suttas, understand the terms, get to the bottom of, like, the key Pali terms, so that you have your own clear view as to what that means, and you can explain to somebody else.

127
00:11:37,866 --> 00:11:51,979
[SPEAKER_02]: But at the same time, maintain the reference point as to, okay, I'm doing this and keeping an eye onto my overall ability to suffer and act out of lust, act out of aversion, because if these things are understood, this should be diminishing.

128
00:11:52,920 --> 00:12:01,807
[SPEAKER_02]: If I'm truly thinking along the lines of the correct dhamma, after I finish the thinking, the desire will be far away.

129
00:12:01,968 --> 00:12:04,150
[SPEAKER_02]: The possibility of ill will will be far away.

130
00:12:04,923 --> 00:12:06,765
[SPEAKER_02]: Not that I'll be immediately anāgāmī.

131
00:12:06,785 --> 00:12:10,548
[SPEAKER_02]: It can come back if I become careless, but it will be far away.

132
00:12:10,568 --> 00:12:12,250
[SPEAKER_02]: It won't be like five minutes later.

133
00:12:13,091 --> 00:12:14,672
[SPEAKER_02]: Craving is there.

134
00:12:14,873 --> 00:12:15,974
[SPEAKER_02]: Sensual thoughts are there.

135
00:12:16,094 --> 00:12:17,355
[SPEAKER_02]: I was irritated by this.

136
00:12:17,375 --> 00:12:17,555
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

137
00:12:17,695 --> 00:12:22,700
[SPEAKER_02]: They would be the actual samādhi as the result of right thinking along the lines of the right dhamma.

138
00:12:23,870 --> 00:12:28,793
[SPEAKER_01]: You say it's because you're actually living what you're reading.

139
00:12:28,993 --> 00:12:30,534
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you're actually seeing what you're reading.

140
00:12:30,554 --> 00:12:31,534
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, yeah, that's the point.

141
00:12:31,574 --> 00:12:32,475
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you are reading it.

142
00:12:33,115 --> 00:12:36,697
[SPEAKER_02]: You need to transcend the level of, like, I'm reading it for the informational purposes.

143
00:12:36,758 --> 00:12:41,800
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm reading it so to see those things here and now on the level of my own mind, on the level of my own experience.

144
00:12:42,341 --> 00:12:43,802
[SPEAKER_02]: And I said that in all talks, like,

145
00:12:44,642 --> 00:12:49,905
[SPEAKER_02]: I remember that, you know, I would be walking back and forth and, like, you know, contemplating and so on.

146
00:12:50,045 --> 00:12:52,347
[SPEAKER_02]: And then it occurred to me, I was like, what is viññāṇa?

147
00:12:52,367 --> 00:12:53,768
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, it's consciousness.

148
00:12:53,788 --> 00:12:54,768
[SPEAKER_02]: We translate it as consciousness.

149
00:12:55,469 --> 00:12:57,030
[SPEAKER_02]: In this language, it's translated like that.

150
00:12:57,070 --> 00:12:59,051
[SPEAKER_02]: In that language, like, but what is it?

151
00:12:59,851 --> 00:13:00,892
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, I know it's consciousness.

152
00:13:01,532 --> 00:13:02,593
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, yeah, but what is consciousness?

153
00:13:04,823 --> 00:13:13,489
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like... but instead of just... oh, let me find another definition now that I can say that's what consciousness is and, like, shift from one information... oh, viññāṇa.

154
00:13:13,790 --> 00:13:15,111
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah, well, viññāṇa is consciousness.

155
00:13:15,671 --> 00:13:16,331
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, but what's consciousness?

156
00:13:17,232 --> 00:13:19,814
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, consciousness is actually... and then, whatever, adopt some other view.

157
00:13:19,834 --> 00:13:25,098
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, well, but that's still not, like, telling me what consciousness is here and now.

158
00:13:25,158 --> 00:13:29,641
[SPEAKER_02]: Those are just, like, passing information from one to the other to the other onto the next information.

159
00:13:29,661 --> 00:13:31,683
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, so what is consciousness?

160
00:13:31,743 --> 00:13:33,284
[SPEAKER_02]: When I say consciousness, consciousness,

161
00:13:34,377 --> 00:13:35,257
[SPEAKER_02]: What am I referring to?

162
00:13:35,277 --> 00:13:38,758
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, when I say left arm, I know I'm referring to this thing.

163
00:13:40,199 --> 00:13:46,160
[SPEAKER_02]: But when I say consciousness, what am I referring to in this space and time of my experience as a whole?

164
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,481
[SPEAKER_02]: What am I referring to when I say consciousness?

165
00:13:50,161 --> 00:13:53,002
[SPEAKER_02]: And then I thought, oh, well, that's a good question.

166
00:13:53,022 --> 00:13:55,103
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, hold on, let's take something simpler.

167
00:13:55,823 --> 00:13:57,583
[SPEAKER_02]: When I say rūpa, what am I referring to?

168
00:13:58,884 --> 00:14:00,564
[SPEAKER_02]: When I say vedanā, what am I referring to?

169
00:14:01,064 --> 00:14:01,584
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, it's feeling.

170
00:14:01,764 --> 00:14:02,625
[SPEAKER_02]: What am I referring to?

171
00:14:02,665 --> 00:14:03,485
[SPEAKER_02]: Where is that thing?

172
00:14:04,344 --> 00:14:05,824
[SPEAKER_02]: Can I describe it in any other terms?

173
00:14:07,845 --> 00:14:09,345
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you understand it.

174
00:14:09,405 --> 00:14:09,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

175
00:14:09,845 --> 00:14:12,765
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how you get to see the actual phenomena that you are referring to.

176
00:14:13,226 --> 00:14:14,066
[SPEAKER_01]: Without having...

177
00:14:14,106 --> 00:14:16,166
[SPEAKER_02]: Without having to adopt another external standpoint.

178
00:14:16,186 --> 00:14:16,566
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

179
00:14:16,646 --> 00:14:17,266
[SPEAKER_01]: Explanation.

180
00:14:17,366 --> 00:14:17,946
[SPEAKER_01]: Explanation.

181
00:14:18,627 --> 00:14:27,648
[SPEAKER_02]: For as long as you're not seeing those phenomena as livingly designated here and now, so to speak, they are at the arm's length.

182
00:14:27,748 --> 00:14:29,268
[SPEAKER_02]: They are from the external standpoint.

183
00:14:30,849 --> 00:14:31,749
[SPEAKER_02]: They are theoretical.

184
00:14:31,969 --> 00:14:32,769
[SPEAKER_02]: They are abstract.

185
00:14:33,885 --> 00:14:42,315
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if you know in and out the etymology, the root, the language, what it refers to, you can compare it to six different takes on it.

186
00:14:43,176 --> 00:14:48,262
[SPEAKER_02]: For you, it's still not pertaining to the right level and it's still not freeing you from suffering.

187
00:14:49,023 --> 00:14:52,307
[SPEAKER_02]: And it never will, because no amount of information can bridge that gap.

188
00:14:54,129 --> 00:14:54,349
[SPEAKER_02]: So...

189
00:14:55,677 --> 00:15:14,092
[SPEAKER_02]: Learning is necessary, but learning will be a lot more efficient, a lot less likely to become proliferating in these abstractly theoretical ways that are confused for the, oh, this is the truth that I know now, although I'm not free from suffering, if it's paired with the actual mental training.

190
00:15:14,553 --> 00:15:18,336
[SPEAKER_02]: Withdrawal from the unwholesome actions, taming of the mind.

191
00:15:19,176 --> 00:15:24,441
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, you wouldn't need even half of the information to see the things

192
00:15:25,310 --> 00:15:27,512
[SPEAKER_02]: That those very suttas talk about.

193
00:15:28,972 --> 00:15:29,393
[SPEAKER_02]: Viññāṇa.

194
00:15:29,913 --> 00:15:30,694
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you see viññāṇa?

195
00:15:32,555 --> 00:15:33,796
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you see the aggregate of viññāṇa?

196
00:15:34,276 --> 00:15:35,137
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, for yourself.

197
00:15:35,737 --> 00:15:41,121
[SPEAKER_02]: Not, do you see another take, someone else's writing of what viññāṇa is.

198
00:15:41,261 --> 00:15:45,303
[SPEAKER_02]: When I see viññāṇa, I think about someone else's words.

199
00:15:45,324 --> 00:15:49,526
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, no, do I see that phenomenon without using any words?

200
00:15:49,586 --> 00:15:51,808
[SPEAKER_02]: Do I just, in the same sense, like, I say my left arm?

201
00:15:51,888 --> 00:15:54,029
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't need to keep saying to myself what my left arm is.

202
00:15:54,129 --> 00:15:54,670
[SPEAKER_02]: I see it.

203
00:15:55,708 --> 00:15:57,889
[SPEAKER_02]: Mentally, I recognize it, what it is.

204
00:15:58,169 --> 00:15:59,910
[SPEAKER_01]: Even in dukkha, suffering?

205
00:16:00,270 --> 00:16:01,130
[SPEAKER_02]: Anything.

206
00:16:01,190 --> 00:16:02,931
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's the whole point.

207
00:16:03,531 --> 00:16:05,312
[SPEAKER_02]: It occurred to me in regard to viññāṇa, I remember.

208
00:16:05,332 --> 00:16:07,313
[SPEAKER_02]: But then, exactly, so what is dukkha, then?

209
00:16:08,333 --> 00:16:09,233
[SPEAKER_02]: What is tantra, then?

210
00:16:12,154 --> 00:16:12,955
[SPEAKER_02]: Being free from suffering?

211
00:16:12,975 --> 00:16:14,075
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, what is that?

212
00:16:14,355 --> 00:16:15,216
[SPEAKER_02]: What do I mean by that?

213
00:16:16,336 --> 00:16:20,298
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, I have a vague notion of freedom, but what does that actually entail for me?

214
00:16:20,378 --> 00:16:21,278
[SPEAKER_02]: What do I think it is?

215
00:16:22,570 --> 00:16:23,431
[SPEAKER_02]: Am I clear on that?

216
00:16:23,471 --> 00:16:37,901
[SPEAKER_02]: Or I'm just, like, just going along with this, like, half-baked, unclarified, mostly obscured ideas that came on account of even the right information, but then never, like, pushed further internally?

217
00:16:38,021 --> 00:16:43,184
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not just like, can I explain tanhā, dukkha or consciousness in some words?

218
00:16:45,846 --> 00:16:48,608
[SPEAKER_02]: Can I see it for myself without even explaining it?

219
00:16:48,888 --> 00:16:51,070
[SPEAKER_02]: When I say tanhā, can I immediately see what that refers to?

220
00:16:52,922 --> 00:16:54,844
[SPEAKER_02]: And it says, citta, can I see what that refers to?

221
00:16:55,645 --> 00:17:00,890
[SPEAKER_02]: So the suttas become a very different ballgame then, if that becomes your standard of understanding.

222
00:17:02,451 --> 00:17:08,237
[SPEAKER_02]: Everything that you've been taking for granted and reading, because it makes sense, it's written, and there is a context you follow, you understand it on some level.

223
00:17:08,718 --> 00:17:09,118
[SPEAKER_01]: It's easy.

224
00:17:09,158 --> 00:17:10,900
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's like, oh, but now...

225
00:17:12,567 --> 00:17:19,453
[SPEAKER_02]: Obviously, this addresses the training in higher mind, the five types of thoughts, that everybody thinks they can do it.

226
00:17:20,514 --> 00:17:21,695
[SPEAKER_02]: Just think the opposite and do this.

227
00:17:21,715 --> 00:17:25,138
[SPEAKER_02]: But the whole thing applies to the higher mind training.

228
00:17:25,438 --> 00:17:26,819
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, okay, so the higher citta.

229
00:17:27,400 --> 00:17:28,821
[SPEAKER_02]: So, am I even seeing the lower citta?

230
00:17:29,381 --> 00:17:32,304
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, when it says citta, do I see that phenomenon of citta here and now?

231
00:17:33,484 --> 00:17:36,310
[SPEAKER_02]: I just have a vague idea about it that pleases me and makes sense.

232
00:17:37,311 --> 00:17:38,714
[SPEAKER_02]: And then I build on top of that.

233
00:17:39,496 --> 00:17:43,844
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, oh, so then I got to see the mind and then this whole...

234
00:17:45,028 --> 00:17:49,270
[SPEAKER_02]: Everything else that follows in the sutta will probably read very differently then, if I see the citta.

235
00:17:49,670 --> 00:17:57,934
[SPEAKER_02]: You'll probably have a lot more acute, direct, efficient instruction than just, oh, think the opposite and hope for the best type of thing.

236
00:17:58,434 --> 00:18:00,114
[SPEAKER_02]: Wish love to everybody and so on.

237
00:18:00,275 --> 00:18:06,117
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, no, it's like directly seeing the citta, and then when he has these thoughts, you also provide this context.

238
00:18:06,257 --> 00:18:08,318
[SPEAKER_02]: When he has those thoughts, well, you provide this context.

239
00:18:09,797 --> 00:18:10,298
[SPEAKER_02]: On that level.

240
00:18:10,478 --> 00:18:12,000
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, oh, it's getting too tight?

241
00:18:12,180 --> 00:18:12,740
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm loosening it.

242
00:18:12,760 --> 00:18:13,341
[SPEAKER_02]: It's getting too loose?

243
00:18:13,401 --> 00:18:13,942
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm tightening it.

244
00:18:14,202 --> 00:18:15,424
[SPEAKER_01]: Tune is becoming perfect.

245
00:18:15,504 --> 00:18:16,685
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're understanding the mind.

246
00:18:16,965 --> 00:18:17,326
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course.

247
00:18:17,406 --> 00:18:19,208
[SPEAKER_02]: And the mind is calming down as a result of it.

248
00:18:20,009 --> 00:18:26,336
[SPEAKER_02]: And as it says, you know, the calm mind, the joy arises, the joy, the clear seeing, the understanding, the liberation.

249
00:18:27,870 --> 00:18:32,955
[SPEAKER_02]: The problem is, obviously, the informational knowledge can be very convincing.

250
00:18:33,015 --> 00:18:35,277
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, you end up... Of course, yeah.

251
00:18:35,778 --> 00:18:43,985
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you end up very convinced and safe in your explanatory system of ideas, of satisfying ideas that provide safety.

252
00:18:45,627 --> 00:18:46,608
[SPEAKER_02]: And you know the answer to everything.

253
00:18:47,228 --> 00:18:48,229
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you read all the suttas.

254
00:18:48,930 --> 00:18:50,171
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's this, and this, and this, and this.

255
00:18:51,248 --> 00:19:01,493
[SPEAKER_02]: So, this internal level of your intention in regard to that, and everything else we talk about, will also become part of something that you, through psychologization, explain back, and have answers for.

256
00:19:01,513 --> 00:19:04,334
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, so why would I... why would I need to...

257
00:19:04,494 --> 00:19:06,515
[SPEAKER_01]: I already understood it, I have an answer for it.

258
00:19:07,856 --> 00:19:14,739
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you need to... that person, any person, needs to be transparent enough to recognize, yeah, but... and ask themselves, but am I free from suffering?

259
00:19:15,519 --> 00:19:16,800
[SPEAKER_02]: Without having to explain anything.

260
00:19:17,080 --> 00:19:19,641
[SPEAKER_02]: Without having to do anything, is the suffering still touching me or not?

261
00:19:20,845 --> 00:19:24,747
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if that's absent, no amount of information can make you open your eyes.

262
00:19:24,887 --> 00:19:27,089
[SPEAKER_02]: It will just distract you further, so to speak.

263
00:19:27,329 --> 00:19:28,530
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what... That's what Yanavera...

264
00:19:28,590 --> 00:19:38,015
[SPEAKER_02]: I think Yanavera said in one of the letters, when apparently some logician was given suttas to read, and he said, well, this weird, like, this is all just mere tautology.

265
00:19:38,836 --> 00:19:41,017
[SPEAKER_02]: Because he could not conceive that this is to be done.

266
00:19:41,838 --> 00:19:48,402
[SPEAKER_02]: He was approaching everything through information of A, B, C. So, if it's this, then it's this, then it's that.

267
00:19:48,542 --> 00:19:49,002
[SPEAKER_02]: I read it.

268
00:19:49,442 --> 00:19:50,143
[SPEAKER_02]: Sutta's finished.

269
00:19:52,931 --> 00:19:55,453
[SPEAKER_02]: So, he thought suttas are just self-explanatory statements.

270
00:19:56,614 --> 00:19:58,956
[SPEAKER_02]: Which, if you don't have the idea of training, they are.

271
00:20:00,957 --> 00:20:02,138
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, you'll be free from ignorance.

272
00:20:02,659 --> 00:20:05,341
[SPEAKER_02]: You'll be free from avijja when you make the effort and free yourself from avijja.

273
00:20:07,503 --> 00:20:09,084
[SPEAKER_01]: Aha, okay, well, thank you.

274
00:20:09,204 --> 00:20:09,924
[SPEAKER_02]: I know that now.

275
00:20:10,125 --> 00:20:11,466
[SPEAKER_02]: Therefore, my work is complete.

276
00:20:12,374 --> 00:20:12,994
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, no.

277
00:20:13,455 --> 00:20:14,175
[SPEAKER_02]: Have you trained?

278
00:20:14,275 --> 00:20:16,536
[SPEAKER_02]: Have you done the things in between, so to say?

279
00:20:16,956 --> 00:20:23,520
[SPEAKER_02]: But if even the things in between, the training, is taken as self-explanatory statements, then you're never going to take on anything.

280
00:20:24,781 --> 00:20:38,108
[SPEAKER_02]: And then even if you take on the practices of keeping the precepts and training and stuff, but still not using that internal living criteria of can I be touched by suffering, you know, whether I have a perfect explanation and technique to manage it or not,

281
00:20:39,427 --> 00:20:55,355
[SPEAKER_02]: You will fall again into one view or the other that would be semi-abstract, semi-psychology, kind of psychologizing these layers of avoiding responsibility, covering it up, building up a system that provides safety through explanation.

282
00:20:55,395 --> 00:20:58,617
[SPEAKER_02]: Because familiarity of explanation, that gets confused for understanding.

283
00:20:59,877 --> 00:21:01,998
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, it makes sense.

284
00:21:02,018 --> 00:21:03,039
[SPEAKER_02]: I, pleases me.

285
00:21:03,959 --> 00:21:06,821
[SPEAKER_02]: Not like, makes sense, I can't be touched by suffering.

286
00:21:07,657 --> 00:21:08,938
[SPEAKER_02]: Make sense equals pleases me.

287
00:21:09,198 --> 00:21:11,419
[SPEAKER_02]: Therefore, or what pleases me equals makes sense.

288
00:21:15,541 --> 00:21:20,083
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's fine to, like, as I said, you can't just switch it off.

289
00:21:20,503 --> 00:21:21,883
[SPEAKER_02]: The tendency of thinking like that.

290
00:21:23,364 --> 00:21:34,129
[SPEAKER_02]: But, you certainly can make the effort and not lose the sight of the bigger picture, which is, sure, I'm contemplating, I'm collecting information, I'm connecting dots in space and time of how things work.

291
00:21:34,329 --> 00:21:34,609
[SPEAKER_02]: But,

292
00:21:35,887 --> 00:21:40,531
[SPEAKER_02]: Also, to bring it back home on the level of in the scene, the scene is seen.

293
00:21:40,952 --> 00:21:41,272
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

294
00:21:42,072 --> 00:21:51,441
[SPEAKER_02]: There is no other loops of having to add extra tidbits of knowledge to make sense, because it's already understood on the level it has arisen.

295
00:21:53,062 --> 00:22:02,070
[SPEAKER_02]: And that can only be done if that criteria and the widest reference point is understanding leads to freedom from suffering, freedom from a liability to suffer.

296
00:22:03,010 --> 00:22:19,275
[SPEAKER_02]: Understanding anything, understanding rūpa, understanding the term viññāṇa, understanding the term dukkha, understanding the term vedanā, pick a term, any aggregate, noble truth, whatever, understand it, that means you are unable to suffer even if you want to.

297
00:22:20,661 --> 00:22:26,884
[SPEAKER_02]: So that criteria must remain clarified and acknowledged throughout.

298
00:22:27,184 --> 00:22:33,607
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's what will keep you in check when you go through your ramblings and roaming mentally and, you know, habits.

299
00:22:34,007 --> 00:22:36,008
[SPEAKER_02]: This will still keep you relatively aligned.

300
00:22:37,288 --> 00:22:38,669
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you dismiss that, then...

